+## Player, lyrics, and live search
+
+Now Playing fills the app viewport with the current artwork behind it. It opens
+the modal once without requesting display fullscreen or closing/reopening it to
+repair a layer. The old Display fullscreen preference and automatic fullscreen
+handoff are retired, including previously saved enabled values. Opening, closing,
+or destroying the player does not enter or exit a host-owned fullscreen session.
+Minimize, maximize/restore, and close remain visible on supported desktop builds.
+Pull down over the header or artwork to return to the prior tab and scroll
+position. Swipe opening/closing follows your finger, commits when released
+past 72 px, and returns if you reverse or release early. Tap expansion takes
+340 ms; closing takes 280 ms. Reopening cancels an old dismissal safely.
+The modal root remains fully opaque and untransformed. Use the window maximize
+button if Spotify itself is not filling your display.
+
+The top-center player header has no text. Title and artist appear below the art.
+Three separate Now Playing size sliders are directly below Player artwork size
+in Settings: normal text, lyrics, and controls. They start at 100% of your app
+sizes; artwork has its own slider. On upgrade, lyrics inherit the old player
+text setting once, preserving their previous size. Later changes are independent.
+Player scrollbars stay hidden even when content needs scrolling.
+In landscape, larger artwork and smaller controls share the left column; live
+lyrics occupy the right column. Portrait stacks them, with normal scrolling.
+Artwork starts at 135% of the previous layout's base size: up to 486 CSS pixels
+with lyrics enabled on a roomy display, bounded by the column width. Settings →
+**Player artwork size** adjusts it from 60–200%, with lyrics on or off. The record
+column scrolls when a larger cover plus controls cannot fit vertically. Controls
+retain their 420-pixel maximum in lyric mode and at least 44-pixel touch targets.
+Settings → **Auto-show available lyrics** enables lookup while the pane starts
+hidden for each song. It appears after lyrics load for that recording. The
+player’s Lyrics button overrides this song only, including an unavailable-lyrics
+pane when manually requested. Older global player On/Off flags migrate to the
+new availability policy; new explicit auto-Off settings remain saved. Timed lines seek to
+their real millisecond timestamps and respect playback restrictions. Untimed
+lyrics remain readable without inventing timing, and unavailable lyrics show a
+message and a Reload lyrics action in the ⋯ menu.
+
+Spotify is the first lyric source. **Broader lyric lookup** adds LRCLIB exact
+lookup, then structured and keyword searches. Search removes release/guest-credit
+clutter, uses the lead artist, and compares recording title, artist, version and
+duration. Search results need a duration within two seconds for timed seeking;
+nearby matches can supply text without claiming synchronization. Incompatible
+live/remix versions, unrelated artists and very different durations are rejected.
+
+If these lookups have no text, **Additional text lyrics** tries lyrics.ovh. Its
+results are labelled text-only and cannot seek. This fallback requires Broader
+lyric lookup to be enabled. Turn Broader lyric lookup off to use Spotify alone.
+LRCLIB receives title, artist, album and duration; lyrics.ovh receives cleaned
+title and lead artist. No Spotify credentials or cookies are sent. LRCLIB also
+receives a public client identification header. Requests are bounded/cancellable,
+LRCLIB requests are spaced, and Retry-After is honored. Results stay in memory
+for up to 32 tracks. Coverage and live provider availability are not guaranteed.
+
+Live search starts after a 100 ms pause. The installed client’s search query is
+preferred when exposed, with the Spotify Web API as fallback. Preview and full
+results share in-flight requests and a 32-page, 60-second cache. Enter renders
+LiquidTouch results with filters and paging. If both service routes fail, the
+page offers Retry and Open desktop search instead of an empty surface. Stale
+responses cannot replace a newer query. Search results still require a working
+Spotify session; the extension does not bypass catalog authorization.
+
+Keyboard keys start about 45% taller at Interface 100%, with larger labels and
+the same horizontal layout. **Keyboard height** adjusts 100–180%; the final height
+is capped to keep the search field visible. Live previews use only the space
+above the keyboard and yield when that space is too small; Enter still opens
+full results. The preview cannot cover the keyboard or its Hide button.
+Pressed keys grow in place and show a readable popup. Input still occurs once
+on release; cancelled touches do not type. The popup ignores pointer input and
+cleans up after release, cancellation, keyboard dismissal or focus loss.
+
+Horizontal swipes now work across page content as well as the bottom navigation.
+A rightward swipe starting in the left 64–120 px goes Back; native detail pages
+also accept a rightward Back swipe from their content. Quick-search pulls can
+start across the full width of the upper region, at least 180 pixels deep and
+up to 280 pixels depending on display/toolbar size, when the page is at its top.
+The toolbar and a 36-pixel band below it also work on scrolled pages. Native
+caption buttons, fields and sliders are excluded. Artwork/mini-player horizontal swipes still skip tracks. Ordinary
+vertical lists, lyric scrolling, sliders, and text fields retain their native
+interaction.
+
+Normal browsing avoids replacing unchanged grids or cards under a finger.
+Loaded Home, Library, search landing and the last results view reuse detached
+page content when their data is unchanged. This preserves card nodes and scroll
+positions. Catalog rescans are deferred until after the tab switch; swipe settling
+lasts 100 ms. Superseded in-flight Spotify routes cannot undo the latest tab,
+while genuine redirects and Back remain supported. This removes extension work
+from the switch; Spotify’s own cold-start/network time is not guaranteed.
+Card surfaces keep their translucent highlights without a separate live blur
+per card. Wallpaper blur is reduced, duplicate gesture handlers are removed,
+and startup library loading is deferred until the touch shell is interactive.
+Page/content transitions last 100–160 ms. The slower player reveal runs only
+while opening, closing or dragging; it has no ongoing idle animation.
+Reduce Motion skips the settle animations. Navigation and teardown still close
+modals synchronously to prevent stale callbacks changing a later page.
+Swipe content follows the finger without a progress indicator or release label.
+Release past the threshold to commit, or reverse/release early to spring back.
+All gestures start at the same normal distance (100%), adjustable in 5% steps.
+Horizontal swipes across the expanded player change tracks, while edge Back wins.
+Downward sheet pulls work from content at its top; scrolled content still scrolls.
+No short-flick velocity shortcut is used. Slower deliberate swipes have up to
+2.5 seconds to finish. Expanded-player dismissal can start across the full width
+of its generous upper area, including space below the toolbar. Native caption
+buttons and sliders keep their own interaction. Window maximize/restore keeps the native OS behavior,
+with matching caption feedback; the player follows the resized layout without
+replaying its opening animation. These
+changes use transforms/opacity; Spotify’s network/startup time remains outside
+the extension’s control.
+
+### Correcting a song’s lyric timing
+
+Open **⋯ → Timing…** in the lyric panel, or Settings → Lyrics → **Current song’s lyric timing**.
+If lyrics arrive late, tap **Earlier**. Each tap advances the displayed lyrics by
+0.25 seconds; **Later** does the opposite. **Reset** returns to the source’s
+original timestamps. Corrections are bounded to 15 seconds either way.
+
+For a larger mismatch, choose **Match a line**, then tap the line currently being
+sung. Timing controls collapse to give the lyrics room while matching. That calibrates the timestamp without seeking or pausing the song. Normal
+lyric taps use the corrected media time afterward. The adjustment is saved on
+this device for the exact Spotify recording URI and source (Spotify or LRCLIB),
+so fixing one version of “Billie Jean” does not shift “Nothing Can Change This
+Love” or a different recording. Only URI/source/offset/time records are stored;
+lyrics themselves remain in the in-memory cache.
+
+Highlight timing reads the player’s current clock every 80 ms while the player
+and lyrics are visible. The clock stops when the view is closed, lyrics are off,
+or the document is hidden. Highlighting changes at the timestamp; auto-scrolling
+settles in 120 ms and yields to user scrolling. Duration checks reject backup
+records differing by more than two seconds. These changes reduce UI lag but do
+not infer a universal correction from a song title: timestamp/source or recording
+mismatches still need an offset or the matching recording.
+
+See **PATCHNOTES.txt** for the numbered list corresponding to the reported issues.
+
+## Listening history and recent searches
+
+Settings → **Listening history** opens a full page with song/artist/album/date
+search, day groups, local timestamps, and tap-to-replay rows. It records observed
+track starts while the extension runs. Pause/resume does not duplicate a play;
+looping the same song is recorded again when its end-to-start transition is
+observed. A track already playing at startup is timestamped when first observed.
+Earlier Spotify plays are not backfilled.
+
+**Listening history defaults to Unlimited**, with optional saved-play limits and
+a confirmed Clear history action in Settings → History & help. A selected cap
+replaces the oldest play when a new one arrives. History uses IndexedDB and the
+account namespace when available. Storage pressure never silently trims history;
+failed saves show a message. **50 search items** and **30 search terms** remain in
+local browser storage. Clearing Spotify’s app/browser data removes local records.
+The package does not upload it or read authentication tokens. Reinstalling just
+the theme/extension keeps these local keys. Searching results and opening/playing
+an item adds it to Recent searches. Artist, song, album, and playlist
+cards play on one tap and open on two; their explicit **Open** button and keyboard
+activation also open the detail page.
+
